The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (parse_dcc(data, data_limit, &dcc_ip,
               &dcc_port, &addr_beg_p, &addr_end_p)) {
    pr_debug("unable to parse dcc command\n");
    continue;
}
...
if ((tuple->src.u3.ip != dcc_ip &&
     ct->tuplehash[!dir].tuple.dst.u3.ip != dcc_ip) ||
    dcc_port == 0) {
    net_warn_ratelimited("Forged DCC command from %pI4: %pI4:%u\n",
                         &tuple->src.u3.ip,
                         &dcc_ip, dcc_port);
    continue;
}
```
Reason: After matching a DCC command and advancing the data pointer, if parsing fails or a forged DCC is detected, the loop continues with the next iteration. However, the data pointer has already been moved past the matched protocol string, and data_limit remains fixed at the original buffer boundary. Subsequent loop iterations then scan beyond the intended data region, potentially causing an out-of-bounds read.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (parse_dcc(data, data_limit, &dcc_ip,
               &dcc_port, &addr_beg_p, &addr_end_p)) {
    pr_debug("unable to parse dcc command\n");
    goto out;
}
...
if ((tuple->src.u3.ip != dcc_ip &&
     ct->tuplehash[!dir].tuple.dst.u3.ip != dcc_ip) ||
    dcc_port == 0) {
    net_warn_ratelimited("Forged DCC command from %pI4: %pI4:%u\n",
                         &tuple->src.u3.ip,
                         &dcc_ip, dcc_port);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason: By jumping to the `out` label instead of continuing the loop, the function exits immediately after detecting an invalid DCC command. This stops all further iteration and avoids reading beyond the buffer. The spinlock is still released at `out`, ensuring proper synchronization and a safe return.

